WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.714
Start with the public boundary: expose only the
routes clients need, apply common gateway

00:00:05.714 --> 00:00:08.475
protections and restrict management access.

00:00:08.475 --> 00:00:12.263
Then examine what happens after traffic enters the
cluster.

00:00:12.263 --> 00:00:17.013
A gateway reduces exposure but does not make
internal callers trustworthy.

00:00:17.013 --> 00:00:23.305
Distinguish a client application credential from the
identity and permissions of the user behind a

00:00:23.305 --> 00:00:23.819
request.

00:00:23.819 --> 00:00:28.355
Follow the certificate trust decision before
discussing encryption.

00:00:28.355 --> 00:00:33.636
The client must verify the intended service, not
merely receive a certificate.

00:00:33.636 --> 00:00:39.188
Mutual TLS adds client-side credentials to the
channel and can identify workloads.

00:00:39.188 --> 00:00:42.980
It still leaves application authorization to be
decided.

00:00:42.980 --> 00:00:49.209
Key protection, certificate renewal and recovery are
ongoing architectural responsibilities,

00:00:49.209 --> 00:00:51.376
especially when pinning is used.

00:00:51.419 --> 00:00:54.323
Containment starts with a communication map.

00:00:54.323 --> 00:00:59.801
Allow required paths and consider outbound traffic
as carefully as inbound traffic.

00:00:59.801 --> 00:01:06.401
Namespaces organize resources, while supported
network policies enforce selected network
boundaries.

00:01:06.401 --> 00:01:10.228
Monitoring and quotas add detection and resource
controls.

00:01:10.228 --> 00:01:16.696
Evaluate each mechanism by the consequence it
limits, and do not mistake an alert or configuration

00:01:16.696 --> 00:01:18.874
object for guaranteed prevention.
